// ============================================================================
// Exploring AI Model Calls in AppDependencies
// ============================================================================
//
// WHAT YOU WILL LEARN:
//   - How Foundry logs AI model calls (chat completions) to AppDependencies
//   - How to read the gen_ai.* properties that contain model details
//   - How to extract model name, operation type, and token counts
//   - How to use "extend" with "tostring()" and "toint()" to parse properties
//
// BACKGROUND:
//   When your Foundry agent calls an AI model (e.g., gpt-5.1, DeepSeek),
//   the OpenTelemetry instrumentation captures that call as a "dependency".
//   The call details — model name, tokens used, etc. — are stored inside
//   the "Properties" column as key-value pairs following the OpenTelemetry
//   GenAI semantic conventions (gen_ai.*).
//
// WHERE TO RUN:
//   Azure Portal → Log Analytics workspace → Logs
// ============================================================================


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 1: Browse raw AppDependencies data
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Let's start by looking at what's in AppDependencies.

AppDependencies
| where TimeGenerated > ago(24h)
| take 5

// Notice the "Properties" column — it's a dynamic (JSON-like) column.
// That's where the AI-specific details live.


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 2: Find only the AI model calls
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Foundry stores a property called "gen_ai.system" for AI-related calls.
// We can use this to filter down to just the model calls.

AppDependencies
| where TimeGenerated > ago(24h)
| where Properties has "gen_ai.system"
| take 10

// The "has" keyword checks if the text appears anywhere in the column.
// This is a quick way to find GenAI-related dependency records.


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 3: Extract the gen_ai properties into readable columns
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The Properties column is a dynamic (JSON) object. We use "extend" with
// "tostring()" to pull out individual values.

AppDependencies
| where TimeGenerated > ago(24h)
| where Properties has "gen_ai.system"
| extend
    AISystem    = tostring(Properties["gen_ai.system"]),
    Operation   = tostring(Properties["gen_ai.operation.name"]),
    ModelAsked  = tostring(Properties["gen_ai.request.model"]),
    ModelUsed   = tostring(Properties["gen_ai.response.model"])
| project TimeGenerated, Name, AISystem, Operation, ModelAsked, ModelUsed, DurationMs, Success
| take 20

// Now you can clearly see:
//   - AISystem:   "openai" (the provider)
//   - Operation:  "chat" (it was a chat completion call)
//   - ModelAsked: the model you requested (e.g., "gpt-5.1")
//   - ModelUsed:  the model that actually responded


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 4: Extract token usage
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Each AI call reports how many tokens were consumed. This is critical
// for understanding costs and performance.

AppDependencies
| where TimeGenerated > ago(24h)
| where Properties has "gen_ai.system"
| extend
    ModelUsed    = tostring(Properties["gen_ai.response.model"]),
    InputTokens  = toint(Properties["gen_ai.usage.input_tokens"]),
    OutputTokens = toint(Properties["gen_ai.usage.output_tokens"])
| extend TotalTokens = InputTokens + OutputTokens
| project TimeGenerated, ModelUsed, InputTokens, OutputTokens, TotalTokens, DurationMs
| sort by TimeGenerated desc
| take 20


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 5: Which models are being used?
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Let's get a list of all unique models your Foundry agents have called.

AppDependencies
| where TimeGenerated > ago(7d)
| where Properties has "gen_ai.system"
| extend ModelUsed = tostring(Properties["gen_ai.response.model"])
| distinct ModelUsed

// This is useful to verify that your agents are calling the correct models.


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 6: How long do model calls take?
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Let's look at the average response time per model.

AppDependencies
| where TimeGenerated > ago(24h)
| where Properties has "gen_ai.system"
| extend ModelUsed = tostring(Properties["gen_ai.response.model"])
| summarize
    AvgDurationMs = avg(DurationMs),
    MinDurationMs = min(DurationMs),
    MaxDurationMs = max(DurationMs),
    CallCount     = count()
    by ModelUsed

// "summarize" groups rows and calculates aggregations.
// Here we see average, min, max duration, and total call count per model.


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 7: See the target endpoints
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The "Target" column shows which Foundry endpoint was called.

AppDependencies
| where TimeGenerated > ago(24h)
| where Properties has "gen_ai.system"
| extend ModelUsed = tostring(Properties["gen_ai.response.model"])
| project TimeGenerated, Name, Target, ModelUsed, DurationMs
| take 10

// The Target typically shows your Foundry project endpoint, for example:
//   "my-project.services.ai.azure.com"


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 8: Check for failed model calls
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Let's see if any AI model calls have failed.

AppDependencies
| where TimeGenerated > ago(7d)
| where Properties has "gen_ai.system"
| where Success == false
| extend
    ModelUsed = tostring(Properties["gen_ai.response.model"]),
    Operation = tostring(Properties["gen_ai.operation.name"])
| project TimeGenerated, Name, ModelUsed, Operation, ResultCode, DurationMs
| sort by TimeGenerated desc
| take 20

// If you see results here, check the ResultCode column for HTTP status codes:
//   429 = Rate limited (too many requests)
//   401 = Authentication issue
//   500 = Server error


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 9: What does a finish reason tell us?
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The model reports why it stopped generating. Usually "stop" means
// the model finished its response naturally.

AppDependencies
| where TimeGenerated > ago(24h)
| where Properties has "gen_ai.system"
| extend
    ModelUsed     = tostring(Properties["gen_ai.response.model"]),
    FinishReasons = tostring(Properties["gen_ai.response.finish_reasons"])
| summarize count() by ModelUsed, FinishReasons

// Common finish reasons:
//   "stop"         → Model completed its response
//   "length"       → Response was cut off (hit token limit)
//   "tool_calls"   → Model wants to call a tool


// ============================================================================
// RECAP — What You Learned:
//
//   - AppDependencies stores all outbound AI model calls
//   - The "Properties" column contains gen_ai.* attributes:
//       gen_ai.system              → AI provider (e.g., "openai")
//       gen_ai.operation.name      → Operation type (e.g., "chat")
//       gen_ai.request.model       → Model you requested
//       gen_ai.response.model      → Model that responded
//       gen_ai.usage.input_tokens  → Prompt token count
//       gen_ai.usage.output_tokens → Completion token count
//       gen_ai.response.finish_reasons → Why generation stopped
//
//   - Use tostring() and toint() to extract values from Properties
//   - Use "has" to quickly check if a property exists
//   - Use "summarize" to aggregate data (avg, min, max, count)
//
// NEXT: Continue with token usage analysis.
// ============================================================================
